A||||10||dans87gt||3.2 Rebuild-Undersize Crankshaft?||||1
Z||000000||dans87gt||09-16-1999||03:33 PM||||I am currently rebuilding my 2.8 in my 87GT. I am going to rebuild it as a 3.2 stroker, I am wondering if it is ok to use an undersize 981 crankshaft from an autoparts store(0.10 under) or spend the extra money to by a new one from GM. There is quite a price difference between them but I do not want to sacrifice any reliability.<BR>I hope I don't have to pull another V6 out of a Fiero for a long,long time-I have scars to prove it.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||09-16-1999||09:47 PM||||As long as the crank is crack free and straight, a 0.010" undersize is not a problem. I've rebuilt lots of engines with undersized cranks that lasted a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||09-16-1999||09:52 PM||||I'm assuming that 0.10" in your post was a typo...if not, stay away from that crank. I won't go anymore than 0.020".||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-16-1999||11:59 PM||||.010, also called "10 under", is about normal on a rework crank. The bearings of course also have to be correct.... Stock ones Will Not work.<P>If you are going to race it... have the thing magnafluxed again. (assuming it was done in the first place) reject it if it has any cracks at all. Even new cranks should be checked before installed for race apps.... you want to make sure some trucker or dock worker didn't slam the hell out of it in transit.||||reg||2
Z||000004||dans87gt||09-17-1999||07:42 AM||||Sorry, it should be 0.010 under not 0.10 under. Thanks for the reply. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||09-17-1999||08:09 AM||||I bought the new GM crank.  It was nearly $400, but considering its intended use, I wouldn't substitute with an used/ remanufactured crank.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fastonpumpgas||09-17-1999||12:46 PM||||there is nothing wrong with using an used crank , as long as it was checked by a professional, you wil never make enough HP to break it unless you had a bad crank to start with, and that can happen with a new one.  Your crank wil worked fine, have it checked though, dont forget the iron head pistons and the 88 fiero or 90 and newer flywheel, if you have a standard tranny.. I am doing the same right now, let me know how it comes out, I work in a machine shop, so maybe I can help , just ask ..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000007||dans87gt||09-18-1999||08:16 AM||||Thanks for the replies, I wasn't to concerned about breaking the crank but more concerned about spinning a bearing with a 0.010 under crank, it seems every time I hear about a bad 2.8, it has a spun bearing||||reg||1
Z||000008||David||09-18-1999||08:40 PM||||It's usually lack of oil that causes a spun bearing.  The fact that the crank is .010 under won't cause it to have any more bearing problems than a standard size crank.  Even a .030 isn't too bad, but I wouldn't want to use anything more than a .030.  I think that I have a .030 in my 4 cyl and haven't had a bit of problems.  I use a high volume oil pump and have 40 psi at idle with hot oil on a summer day.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||09-18-1999||09:31 PM||||Pinging or predetonation is another cause of premature bearing failure. This wrecks havoc on pistons and rings too. The hammering effect of the predetonation apparently damages the bearing. Once it's out of tolerance, then the oil pressure drops at that journal which reduces the cushion of oil it rides on. Then it's not long before the bearing goes south. Never let your engine ping. If it does, double check the timing and use a better grade of gasoline with a higher octane rating. Carbon deposits in the compustion chamber of high mileage cars can often cause predetonation. Basically the air/fuel mixture is exploding from a glowing bit of carbon before the piston reaches top dead center--very bad. This cause dieseling too (running on after you turn off the ignition).||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-19-1999||12:27 AM||||An "under" crank, with matching bearings, is a non issue for street, and even most race, use. "Spinning" a bearing can only happen if the system wears out the bearing. Wearout can only happen if: (In no real order......)<P>-&gt; the bearing is installed wrong.<BR>-&gt; the bearing doesn't get oiled enough.<BR>-&gt; the bearing is really old.<BR>-&gt; the bearing is abused. (knock, ping, etc, included.)||||reg||11
